  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 228 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nRain has cleared to the southeast as higher pressure has built in \nfrom the north today. Cloud cover will continue to scatter this \nafternoon, with sky conditions becoming mainly clear into tonight. \nTonight's set up looks favorable for radiational fog development for \nmuch of the area due to clear skies, light sfc winds and ground \nmoisture from earlier rainfall. The only possible limiting factor may\nbe a breeze just off the surface, as shown on forecast soundings. \nStill, confidence is high enough to include areas of fog tonight and \nearly Friday. \n\nThe main challenge with this forecast package is then POPs - \ndetermining probabilities and timing.\n\nOn Friday, the H5 flow becomes more zonal across KS while today's \ntrough axis deepens over MO. A shortwave will pass to our north \nacross SD and into MN during the day. Sunshine and return flow at \nsfc will allow temps to be a touch warmer, with highs likely reaching\nthe upper 80s. The aforementioned shortwave may then bring a sfc \nboundary into southern Nebraska Friday night. A few storms could \nform (20-40% chance) along the boundary and track southeast into \nnorthern KS. Elevated instability would allow for thunderstorms, but\nshear does not organized enough to produce severe storms. \n\nThe surface boundary is likely to remain over northern KS on \nSaturday. There is some uncertainty in whether overnight storms \nwill linger into the daytime hours, but if rain and clouds are able \nto clear, the environment could become increasingly favorable for \nstrong to severe thunderstorm development during the afternoon or \nevening. Storms that develop near or just south of the boundary will \ndo so in an environment featuring steep lapse rates and effective \nshear around 40 kts. Damaging wind gusts and small hail would be the \nmain hazards.\n\nA progressive pattern has the sfc boundary lifting northward into\nsouthern Nebraska on Sunday as a warm front. Aloft, the H5 ridge \nover the south-central US may begin to transition to the east as a \nstrong Pacific Northwest system progresses eastward across the\nnorthern CONUS. That would allow flow over the central US to shift \nfrom westerly to southwesterly. Theta-e advection will create an \nunstable air mass, however, a cap and lack of forcing will likely \nkeep conditions dry from Sunday afternoon into midday Monday. While \nthe Pacific Northwest system will remain well north of the area, an \nassociated sfc cold front looks to slide into northern KS by Monday \nnight. Thunderstorm chances again increase Monday night and more so \non Tuesday as the boundary interacts with the moist and unstable air \nmass. Will keep POPs going into Wednesday until subsidence from high \npressure begins to limit vertical motion north of the front.",
